<http://purl.org/nanopub/temp/np1/assertion> {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/organism-cytochrome-c-predicted-divergence> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/organism-cytochrome-c-predicted-divergence> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Across three ESMFold predictions of cytochrome c, the pairs rank the same way by sequence substitutions and by CA RMSD after superposition (Bos taurus vs Thunnus alalunga: 16 substitutions in 103 aligned positions, CA RMSD 0.42 angstrom; Bos taurus vs Saccharomyces cerevisiae (strain ATCC 204508 / S288c): 37 substitutions in 104 aligned positions, CA RMSD 1.67 angstrom; Thunnus alalunga vs Saccharomyces cerevisiae (strain ATCC 204508 / S288c): 38 substitutions in 103 aligned positions, CA RMSD 3.48 angstrom)." .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/organism-cytochrome-c-predicted-divergence> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/description> "A derived claim over model outputs. The same model folded cytochrome c from three organisms; the predictions were aligned and superposed in pure Node. The pair with fewest substitutions has the smallest RMSD and the pair with most has the largest. This is consistent with the idea that sequence divergence accumulates while the fold is conserved, which is the setting of Kimura's substitution-rate claim, and it is not a test of that claim." .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/organism-cytochrome-c-predicted-divergence> <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/hypothesisType> "derived claim over model outputs" .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/organism-cytochrome-c-predicted-divergence> <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Three predictions by one model (ESMFold v1 through the ESM Atlas endpoint), 2026-10-31T06:44:53.631Z. Alignment and superposition as described in the evidence item. Three proteins give three pairs, so no rate, no fit and no significance can be read from them." .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/organism-cytochrome-c-predicted-divergence> <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/hasEvidence> <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/evidence/organism-cytochrome-c-superposition> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/organism-cytochrome-c-predicted-divergence> <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/proposedTest> "Repeat on a set of orthologs large enough to regress RMSD on substitutions, using experimental structures where they exist, and check whether the ordering survives when predictions are replaced by measured structures." .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/organism-cytochrome-c-predicted-divergence> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Does not establish: Kimura's substitution-rate claim, a molecular clock, or any measured structural difference between these proteins. Both quantities in each pair derive from predictions by a single model, and three points order trivially." .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/organism-cytochrome-c-predicted-divergence> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#seeAlso> <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/kimura-substitution-rate> .
}
